# Security headers, added 2026-08-21 after an assessment of the live site.
#
# The assessment found the important things already sound: no secrets in the
# shipped JavaScript, every anonymous write to the database refused by policy
# (insert and delete both, on every table), and no open redirect in the Steam
# sign in function. What was missing was every response header, which is what
# this file adds.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy is ENFORCING as of 2026-08-21, after a
# Report-Only pass on the live site found no violations on the landing page,
# home or the gallery. It went in report only first, deliberately. A wrong
# CSP takes the site down rather than protecting it, and this one has to allow
# a real spread of third parties: YouTube for the landing film, Discord for
# the presence widget, Steam for avatars, Supabase over both https and
# websockets for the shoutbox. Report-Only publishes the same rules and
# reports violations to the browser console without enforcing them. Once the
# console is clean on the live site, drop the "-Report-Only" from the header
# name below and it starts protecting.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://cdn.discordapp.com https://i.ytimg.com https://i.imgur.com https://*.steamstatic.com https://*.akamaihd.net https://zcpbpcktinlqnxmqddzc.supabase.co; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com https://zcpbpcktinlqnxmqddzc.supabase.co wss://zcpbpcktinlqnxmqddzc.supabase.co https://discord.com; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://www.youtube.com https://discord.com; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'

# Why script-src allows inline, which is weaker than it looks on paper.
#
# Cloudflare injects two things of its own into every page: the Web Analytics
# beacon and the bot challenge platform, both of which run inline scripts whose
# content changes with Cloudflare's versions. Pinning hashes for those means
# the policy breaks silently whenever Cloudflare ships an update, which is the
# worst kind of security control: one that fails without telling anybody.
#
# So script-src is permissive and the value comes from the other directives,
# which still hold: connect-src stops data being posted to a host we did not
# name, frame-src stops arbitrary embeds, object-src and base-uri close two
# classic injection routes, and form-action stops a form being retargeted.
# React escaping remains the real defence against injected markup.
#
# To tighten later: turn off Cloudflare Web Analytics in the dashboard, then
# the only inline script left is the origin guard in index.html and its hash
# can be pinned.
